{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790987763,"nonce":"6r0BOJupiD+JZyrRgRKn17nhRNU=","title":"the desktop test expects cursor Locked to fail on Windows, which winit supports","message":"CI's desktop-windows job fails on its first run (run 37078847873, commit 24ff346): `FAIL cursor grab: Locked gave true` (15 passed, 2 skipped, 1 failed). crates/xwindow-check/tests/desktop.rs puts Win32 with X11 in its support table, `Platform::X11 | Platform::Win32 =\u003e (true, false)`, so it expects `CursorGrab::Locked` to be refused on Windows.\n\nwinit 0.30's docs for CursorGrabMode::Locked list only X11 (not implemented) and iOS/Android as unsupported. On Windows, winit-0.30.13 platform_impl/windows/window.rs set_cursor_grab sets CursorFlags::LOCKED and returns Ok, and window_state.rs confines the cursor to its position while LOCKED is set. The backend reports that correctly; the test's expectation is wrong.\n\nFix: Win32 expects (true, true): `Platform::X11 =\u003e (true, false)`, `Platform::Win32 | Platform::Wayland | Platform::Other =\u003e (true, true)`. Possibly also check, as Wayland's branch does, what the cursor does while locked.","files":null}]}